{"qid":"Q1139762","names":["Empire of Kanem"],"title":"Empire of Kanem","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Empire of Kanem was the first phase of the Kanem–Bornu state in the Lake Chad basin, ruled by the Sayfawa dynasty, which claimed Yemeni Arab descent and had converted to Islam by the late eleventh century. Under Mai Dunama II Dibalemi (died c. 1259), the empire reached its territorial apex, reportedly fielding 40,000 cavalry and extending authority northwest into the Fezzan and eastward to the Nilotic Sudan; Dunama established a hostel for Kanem pilgrims in Cairo, signaling the court's integration into trans-Saharan Islamic networks. Internal succession wars between Dawudid and Idrisid branches of the Sayfawa, combined with raids by the Bulala people who may have been a Sayfawa cadet lineage, progressively weakened the empire. The Bulala captured the capital Njimi around 1380, forcing the Sayfawa under Mai Umar ibn Idris to migrate westward into Bornu, where a second dynastic phase continued.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Kanem's core territory near Lake Chad is attested in Arabic geographical sources and Sayfawa king-lists (diwan), but territorial claims during the imperial apex under Dunama II are likely exaggerated in tradition and cannot be mapped reliably.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kanem–Bornu Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanem%E2%80%93Bornu_Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Kanem-Bornu Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kanem-Bornu"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kanem Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanem_Empire"}],"britannica":"place/Kanem-Bo